        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air Leakage Areas:



Heated Crawspace:



Floor Joist header areas are not seal properly is some places.



Access door is not properly sealed. </airSeal>
                <windows>Upgrade windows to ENERGY STARÂ® Zone 3 rated.



Upgrade back sliding window.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on windows/doors and the criteria for these products.

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>What was originally thought to be a HRV in the crawlspace is actually just a fan coil system that distributes heat from the boiler throughout the house. 



There is currently no fresh air coming into the home besides natural leaks and cracks.



If there are air sealing upgrades completed to the home, it will make it tighter, but it will not allow for the home to breathe as it was before. A mechanical ventilation system such as an HRV/ERV should be installed to allow the home to be properly ventilated. Not installing a system after air sealing can lead to issues with mold and mildew.



The HRV/ERV will recover heated exhaust air via a heat exchanger and preheat supply air with the extracted heat, making the units very efficient. Make sure the unit is ENERGY STARÂ® rated and HVI certified.



HRV = Heat Recovery Ventilator

ERV = Enthalpy Recovery Ventilator



ERV also can control indoor humidity levels as well.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on ventilation systems and the criteria for these products.

</VentilationSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
